A||||5||DaveL||87 2.5 Oil In Air Cleaner||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||12-17-2001||03:55 PM||||Last time I checked my air filter, there was a pool of oil in it. What would cause this? I put a new PCV valve on it, and have suction there at an idle....<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||12-17-2001||04:11 PM||||I seem to remember someone posting that a defective THERMAC system (a.k.a. heated air intake) can cause oil to appear in the air cleaner.  Might be worth checking... ||||reg||5
Z||000002||CanadianGT||12-17-2001||04:22 PM||||Worn rings can also cause this, or a defective PCV system||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jncomutt||12-17-2001||05:04 PM||||I have had 2 instances of this.  Both have already been mentioned.  On my coupe with 186k miles, I get oily filter because the rings are shot.<P>On my 84 with only 55k, I got an oily filter wwhen I switched my air cleaner assembly (the whole metal air filter holder & thermac piece).  I switched back to the orginal and the oil stopped.  So, this is where thought of the faulty thermac came into play.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-17-2001||08:01 PM||||New PCV valve could be bad or wrong one.<P>Other wise it's most likely a developing ring problem.<P>87-88 doesn't have thermac.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||GTDude||12-18-2001||12:24 AM||||87 and 88 2.5's had a very bad problem of the rings carbon seizing on the pistons.  Pull the big hose off that goes to the breather filter on the air filter.  Start the car and rev it up two or three times.  If you feel pressure, either the pcv system is not working correctly...or you have a ring problem.  If you think it's a ring problem repost and I'll give you the details of how to possibly decarbonize the pistons.  Good Luck.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSCF0003a.JPG"> ||||reg||1
